          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My son has been wheezing at night for a month and his blue inhaler is running out fast. Should he be seen urgently, and what does asthma guidance describe about stepping up treatment?",
          "answer": "**Please speak to his doctor soon.** Night wheezing for a month, with a reliever (\"blue\") inhaler running out fast, suggests his asthma may not be well controlled. Asthma guidance and the lifestyle guidance on adherence both describe rising reliever use this way: it means the condition is not under control, not that the reliever is working well. Call the surgery or his clinic this week and say that night wheezing has lasted a month and the reliever is running out fast. A routine check-up alone is probably not the right approach. A quick check of his symptoms found no emergency pattern, but that is not a clearance, because the check only covers a fixed list of presentations.\n\n**Get emergency help now if you see any of these:**\n- Fast breathing, or the skin sucks in between or under the ribs\n- Grunting, or nostrils flaring\n- He can't speak in full sentences, or pauses for breath between words\n- He is too breathless to feed or talk\n- Lips, tongue, face or fingertips look blue or grey\n- He is drowsy, confused, or unusually exhausted\n- He is breathless at rest, or the breathlessness came on suddenly\n- The reliever doesn't help, or symptoms return within a few hours\n- His chest has gone quiet. The guidance treats this as a serious sign, not a reassuring one.\n\n**What the guidance says about stepping up treatment:** Asthma guidance describes adjusting treatment in steps and reviewing it. Before stepping up, a clinician checks inhaler technique and whether medicines are being taken as prescribed, because these often explain what looks like treatment failure. I could not find the thresholds for children in the material I had, and the step-up passages mainly cover adults and adolescents, so his doctor will need to apply the children's guidance. I can't tell you which medicine or dose to use.\n\n**How strong is the evidence?** The material I had does not grade how strong the evidence is for these recommendations. Ask his clinician which guideline they are following and how well supported the chosen step is.\n\n**Where guidance differs:** The global strategy guidance prefers an inhaled corticosteroid with a fast-acting long-acting bronchodilator as the reliever across steps. Some national panel guidance says a short-acting reliever alongside a regular controller is still appropriate in mild disease. I can't say which applies to him. His doctor can explain.\n\n**Questions to ask at the appointment:**\n- Is his inhaler technique right, and is his daily preventer being used as planned?\n- Does he have a written asthma action plan?\n- What should we do if the reliever stops working between appointments?\n\nBring a note of how often he has needed the reliever and how often he wakes at night.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
